<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nehemiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nehemiah was a Jewish official at the Persian court who, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, became governor of the province of Yehud under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-i">Artaxerxes I</a>. Arriving around 445 BCE, he organised the rebuilding of the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> despite opposition from neighbouring leaders, among them Sanballat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geshem-the-arab">Geshem the Arab</a>, and enforced religious reforms. The biblical Book of Nehemiah is based on his memoir.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nehemiah.jpg" alt="Nehemiah" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Russian Icon Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prophet_nehemiah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Codex Ambrosianus in Milan and the Codex Taurinensis in Turin, 193 leaves in five parts, hold passages from the gospels, the Epistles and <b>Nehemiah</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Many non-Persians served the king too: the Egyptian priest and physician Udjahorresnet advised Cambyses II and Darius, and the Jewish courtier <b>Nehemiah</b>, by his own account, was sent to govern Judah.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Achaemenid Empire the Qedarites kept their autonomy; Geshem the Arab, opponent of <b>Nehemiah</b>, and his son Qaynu, named king of Qedar on a silver bowl from Egypt, date to the 5th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/qedarites">Qedarites</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nehemiah</b>, a court official, rebuilt Jerusalem's walls c. 445 BCE, and Ezra brought the law of Moses to the centre of community life.</blockquote><a href="/state/yehud-medinata">Yehud Medinata</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.7,35.31&amp;year=-435" aria-label="Judea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5460 1010 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nehemiah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5910 1280h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5910 1280h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.7,35.31&amp;year=-435">What was here in 435 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ye see the distress that we are in, how Jerusalem lieth waste, and the gates thereof are burned with fire: come, and let us build up the wall of Jerusalem, that we be no more a reproach.&#8221;</p><footer>Nehemiah 2:17, to the people of Jerusalem &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Nehemiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Be not ye afraid of them: remember the LORD, which is great and terrible, and fight for your brethren, your sons, and your daughters, your wives, and your houses.&#8221;</p><footer>Nehemiah 4:14, to the builders of the wall &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Nehemiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am doing a great work, so that I cannot come down: why should the work cease, whilst I leave it, and come down to you?&#8221;</p><footer>Nehemiah 6:3, answering his enemies' summons &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Nehemiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The God of heaven, he will prosper us; therefore we his servants will arise and build…&#8221;</p><footer>Nehemiah 2:20, answering those who mocked the rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; his own memoir as the Book of Nehemiah gives it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Nehemiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Should such a man as I flee? and who is there, that, being as I am, would go into the temple to save his life? I will not go in.&#8221;</p><footer>Nehemiah 6:11, urged to hide in the Temple from those who plotted against him &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; his own memoir as the Book of Nehemiah gives it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Nehemiah" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
